Hill."]},{"type":"news-image","params":{"image":{"id":"tmx_newsfile:98693fa78094b-d677ae3d3c8093991e81443ce579c0d2","source-width":1308,"source-height":485,"bg-color":"#8b8785"}}},{"type":"p","children":[{"type":"i","children":["Figure 1: Panoramic photograph showing widespread mineralization identified in SH26-27."]}]},{"type":"p","children":["To view an enhanced version of this graphic, please visit:"]},{"type":"news-image","params":{"image":{"id":"tmx_newsfile:98693fa78094b-d677ae3d3c8093991e81443ce579c0d2","source-width":1308,"source-height":485,"bg-color":"#8b8785"}}},{"type":"news-image","params":{"image":{"id":"tmx_newsfile:98693fa78094b-445666ad59e7e9a74a771c063807e032","source-width":862,"source-height":658,"bg-color":"#58524e"}}},{"type":"p","children":[{"type":"i","children":["Figure 2: Photograph showing massive to semi massive mineralization from SH26-27."]}]},{"type":"p","children":["To view an enhanced version of this graphic, please visit:"]},{"type":"news-image","params":{"image":{"id":"tmx_newsfile:98693fa78094b-445666ad59e7e9a74a771c063807e032","source-width":862,"source-height":658,"bg-color":"#58524e"}}},{"type":"news-image","params":{"image":{"id":"tmx_newsfile:98693fa78094b-7975b08ffb8f1fe2c40463d658613086","source-width":935,"source-height":605,"bg-color":"#cbccc7"}}},{"type":"p","children":[{"type":"i","children":["Figure 3: Plan view of the Silver Hill project area showing the location of drilled intercepts (AgEq), intercepts with pending assays (black) and drilled trace locations (grey). Note the variability in grade between SH26-19, SH26-26 and SH26-27."]}]},{"type":"p","children":["To view an enhanced version of this graphic, please visit:"]},{"type":"news-image","params":{"image":{"id":"tmx_newsfile:98693fa78094b-7975b08ffb8f1fe2c40463d658613086","source-width":935,"source-height":605,"bg-color":"#cbccc7"}}},{"type":"news-image","params":{"image":{"id":"tmx_newsfile:98693fa78094b-fe9c927254dca4f68378206a4507dc34","source-width":940,"source-height":665,"bg-color":"#ececeb"}}},{"type":"p","children":[{"type":"i","children":["Figure 4: Long section looking southeast (113°) showing intercept locations colored by AgEq. Black dots indicate intercepts with pending assays. Note the variability in grade between SH26-19, SH26-26 and SH26-27."]}]},{"type":"p","children":["To view an enhanced version of this graphic, please visit:"]},{"type":"news-image","params":{"image":{"id":"tmx_newsfile:98693fa78094b-fe9c927254dca4f68378206a4507dc34","source-width":940,"source-height":665,"bg-color":"#ececeb"}}},{"type":"p","children":[{"type":"b","children":["Joe Cullen, CEO of Metalsource Mining, commented:"]}]},{"type":"p","children":[{"type":"i","children":["\"We recognize the importance of accurate technical disclosure and have moved to correct the previously reported figures. The corrected results demonstrate substantially stronger polymetallic grades across the broader 17.28-metre mineralized envelope, further reinforcing the significance of SH26-27."]}]},{"type":"p","children":[{"type":"i","children":["\"It suggests the lower-grade area does not appear to be the southern limit of the system and raises the possibility of additional mineralized zones beyond the footprint we have been concentrating on. Combined with the mineralization visually identified farther south in SH26-45, for which assays remain pending, we now have a compelling area to investigate through further stepout drilling."]}]},{"type":"p","children":[{"type":"i","children":["\"Our objective is not simply to accumulate high-grade intercepts within the historic mine area. It is to understand how large the Silver Hill system could ultimately become. Every successful stepout that identifies mineralization beyond our established drilling footprint helps us evaluate that opportunity. SH26-27 gives us another reason to look farther south, and that is precisely the kind of geological progress we are working to achieve.\""]}]},{"type":"p","children":[{"type":"b","children":["Why This Matters to Investors"]}]},{"type":"p","children":["The significance of SH26-27 extends beyond its high-grade internal intervals. The corrected results demonstrate substantially stronger polymetallic grades across the broader 17.28-metre mineralized envelope than previously reported. By identifying stronger mineralization beyond an intervening lower-grade area, Metalsource has opened a compelling new direction for exploration, suggesting the system may extend beyond its historically recognized footprint."]},{"type":"p","children":[{"type":"b","children":["The return of high-grade gold mineralization farther south is particularly encouraging, raising the possibility of additional higher-grade zones beyond those previously identified."]}," As aggressive stepout drilling continues, Metalsource is systematically pushing into previously untested areas, integrating new results with its evolving geological model to better understand the distribution of mineralization and identify additional targets."]},{"type":"p","children":["Success in extending mineralization along strike and at depth could materially expand Silver Hill's exploration potential. With multiple assays pending, SH26-27 reinforces the Company's strategy of looking beyond the historic mine workings to investigate what could ultimately prove to be a substantially larger polymetallic system."]},{"type":"table","children":[{"type":"table-header","children":[{"type":"table-row","children":[{"type":"table-data-cell","children":[{"type":"b","children":["Drill Hole ID"]}]},{"type":"table-data-cell","children":[{"type":"b","children":["Easting (m)"]}]},{"type":"table-data-cell","children":[{"type":"b","children":["Northing (m)"]}]},{"type":"table-data-cell","children":[{"type":"b","children":["Elev. (m)"]}]},{"type":"table-data-cell","children":[{"type":"b","children":["Azimuth"]}]},{"type":"table-data-cell","children":[{"type":"b","children":["Dip"]}]},{"type":"table-data-cell","children":